 A variant that explicitly performs importance sampling after coarse rendering to refine details. It computes weights from coarse samples, samples new depths from the resulting PDF, and queries the network again. It detaches gradients from the coarse weights to stabilize training [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
+### Occupancy Grid Sampling
+A strategy to select sample points within a predefined sphere radius $r$ for each scene, using an OccupancyGrid to skip empty space. The density is set to zero for points outside the bounding sphere. The sphere radius $r$ is typically 1.5 for nerf-synthetic dataset scenes, and the occupancy threshold is 0.005 for all experiments [S4].
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{rays} \cdot N_{samples})$ for coarse, plus $O(N_{rays} \cdot N_{importance})$ for fine. The estimator update is $O(N_{samples})$ [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rays} \cdot N_{samples})$ for storing intervals and activations. Chunking reduces peak memory for network inference [S3, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
 - **Performance:** Speedups of 1.5× to 20× over standard NeRF rendering depending on the scene and estimator used [S1].
 - **Strengths:** Decoupled sampling allows easy integration of various acceleration structures (Occupancy Grid, Proposal Network) [S1]. Interval-based representation supports anti-aliasing (Mip-NeRF) [S1].
